llm_response:
{"solution": {"warehouse_1": 8, "warehouse_2": 8}, "cost": 52}

Constraints: x + y ≥ 16 (pallets), 1.0x + 0.5y ≤ 12 (dock hours), x, y ≥ 0. Warehouse 1 is cheaper per pallet ($2.50 vs $4.00) but consumes twice the dock time, so the dock capacity binds. At the optimum both constraints are tight: x + y = 16 and x + 0.5y = 12, giving x = 8, y = 8, with cost 8(2.50) + 8(4.00) = $52.00. All 12 dock hours are used and exactly 16 pallets are delivered.

Qualifications: (1) The solution happens to be integer-valued, so no rounding issue arises; if fractional pallets were disallowed and the vertex had been fractional, a small adjustment would be needed. (2) Shipping more than 16 pallets never helps — every extra pallet adds cost and dock time. (3) The plan has zero slack on dock time, so any delay or overrun at the dock puts the delivery at risk; if you want a buffer, note that shifting one pallet from Warehouse 1 to Warehouse 2 (7 and 9) frees 0.5 hour of dock time for an extra $1.50. (4) If dock capacity could be raised to 16 hours, the all-Warehouse-1 plan (16 pallets, $40) would be feasible and would save $12 — worth checking whether extra dock time can be arranged.

